## ABSTRACT
Shaping crystalline porous materials such as metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and zeolites into two-dimensional (2D) nanosheet forms is highly desirable for developing high-performance molecular sieving membranes. However, conventional exfoliation–deposition is complex and challenging for the large-scale fabrication of nanosheet MOF tubular membranes. Here, for the first time, we report a direct growth technique by $Z\mathrm{nO}$ self-conversion and ammonia assistance to fabricate zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) membranes consisting of 2D nanosheets on porous hollow fiber substrates; the membranes are suitable for large-scale industrial gas separation processes. The proposed fabrication process for ZIF nanosheet membranes is based on the localized self-conversion of a pre-deposited thin layer of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ in a ligand solution containing ammonium hydroxide as a modulator. The resulting ZIF 2D nanosheet tubular membrane is highly oriented and only $50\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness. It exhibits excellent molecular sieving performance, with high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance and selectivity for $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ separation. This technique shows great promise in MOF nanosheet membrane fabrication for large-scale molecular sieving applications.
## 1 Introduction
The fabrication of ultrathin two-dimensional (2D) nanosheet membranes for molecular sieving, using 2D nanosheet materials including graphene, graphene oxide (GO), zeolitic imidazole frameworks (ZIFs), and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) as building blocks for highly efficient gas separation, has attracted extensive research interest because these materials offer unique properties originating from their 2D morphologies [1–10]. However, for crystalline membrane materials such as ZIFs and MOFs, the developed preparation methods such as in-situ synthesis [11, 12], seeded growth [13, 14], surface functionalization [15, 16], flow growth [17, 18], and surface special seeding [19, 20] are only suitable for preparing thick membranes and cannot produce 2D nanosheet membranes. Therefore, the preparation of ultrathin crystalline molecular sieving membranes in the form of 2D nanosheets remains a significant challenge.
Until now, few reports [21–26] have discussed the preparation of ultrathin 2D nanosheet crystalline molecular sieving membranes. All investigated methods comprise exfoliation, to obtain the ultrathin crystalline nanosheets, followed by deposition, or the coating of nanosheets to form 2D ZIF or MOF nanosheet membranes on porous plates or disks as substrates. Tsapatsis et al. [22] first demonstrated the fabrication of zeolite molecular sieve membranes on a porous $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ plate as a substrate by the secondary growth of 2D seeds comprising exfoliated nanosheets or sliceshaped zeolites. The multilamellar zeolite was exfoliated by a polymer-melt blending technique. The resulting ultrathin 2D nanosheet zeolite membrane exhibited excellent selectivity for the separation of xylene isomers. Yang et al. [25] fabricated a ${\sim}5{\cdot}\mathrm{nm}$ -thick ultrathin 2D ZIF membrane by a soft-physical exfoliation process; pristine MOF crystals were first wet-ball-milled at a very low speed before exfoliation by ultrasonication in a volatile solvent. In combination with a series of treatments, the nanosheet suspensions were filtered through porous disks as supports to fabricate ultrathin 2D ZIF membranes with superselective $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ molecular sieving properties. Recently, Gascon et al. [26] presented a bottom-up synthesis strategy for highly crystalline, intact MOF nanosheets with good dispersibility in polymer matrices, yielding composite materials with superior $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ separation performance. These reported preparation methods, inevitably involving exfoliation steps, are complex, arduous, and time-consuming. Moreover, shortcomings such as fragmentation, morphological damage, and reaggregation of the detached sheets often occur in these methods. The porous substrates used for fabricating MOF nanosheet membranes thus far have all been either flat or disc-shaped. The exfoliation–deposition process is unsuitable for large-scale fabrication and subsequent industrial application. The deposition of exfoliated nanosheets on tubular substrates is highly desirable, but remains challenging. Therefore, it is necessary to develop a simple and effective method for preparing highly oriented ultrathin 2D nanosheet crystalline molecular sieving membranes.
ZIFs, an important subclass of MOFs that exhibits high thermal and chemical stability, are very attractive for membrane applications. Therefore, a high-performance 2D nanosheet ZIF membrane could achieve excellent molecular sieving of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ which is among the most important technologies for zero-emission fossil fuel power generation and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production [27–29]. Here, we report a novel directgrowth strategy for preparing ultrathin 2D ZIF nanosheet membranes on porous $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hollow-fiber substrates by $Z\mathrm{nO}$ self-conversion with ammonia assistance. The resulting membrane shows excellent molecular sieving properties for $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ separation. To the best of our knowledge, no investigation has yet obtained a nanosheet crystalline membrane through a direct growth method. The present method is based on our hypothesis that $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ -ZIF crystals preferentially nucleate on $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and grow with good orientation into an ultrathin ZIF membrane in the form of a nanosheet. Ammonia acts as a synthetic modulator and provides fine control over both the synthesis ligand solution and the growth conditions. Porous $\mathrm{{Al}_{2}\mathrm{{O}_{3}}}$ tubes enable the fabrication of membranes with substantially higher densities and are more suitable for large-scale industrial applications than flat or disc-shaped substrates.
The ZIF employed here for nanosheet membrane fabrication is $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{benzimidazole})_{4}$ $\left(Z{\bmod{4}}\right)$ , which has already shown potential for molecular sieving membrane applications [25]. $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(b I m)_{4}}$ consists of 2D layers oriented perpendicular to the $c$ axis and stacked via weak van der Waals interactions. $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{bIm})_{4}$ is considered the secondary building unit of the 2D layer; the aperture size is estimated as $0.21{\mathrm{nm}}$ and the distance between adjacent layers is ${\sim}1.18\ \mathrm{nm}$ [25, 30] Considering the structural flexibility of its apertures [31], a $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{bIm})_{4}$ nanosheet grown in the [002] direction could facilitate $0.29\mathrm{-nm}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeation through the membrane. As illustrated in Scheme 1, highly oriented ZIF nanosheet membranes can be obtained simply by the self-conversion of a pre-deposited $Z\mathrm{nO}$ nanolayer in a synthesis solution containing bIm ligands and ammonium hydroxide as a modulator. The $Z\mathrm{nO}$ nanolayer is multifunctional in fabricating the nanosheet membrane, providing a Zn source, localized growth sites, orientational control, and nanosheet– substrate adhesion.
Utilizing our facile growth technique, not only a uniform ZIF nanosheet membrane is obtained, but also the membrane shows preferential growth in the [002] direction, promoting both high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ selectivity.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Preparation and coating of ZnO nanoparticles (NPs) on porous tubes
First, $8.34\mathrm{g}Z\mathrm{n(Ac)}_{2}$ was dissolved in $48\mathrm{mL}$ of ethylene glycol monomethyl ether $(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ EGME, $99.0\%$ ) at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ while stirring. Afterwards, $2.4\mathrm{mL}$ monoethanolamine $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{NO},$ MEA, $99.0\%$ ) was added dropwise to the solution. The solution was aged at room temperature for $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain a $14.3~\mathrm{wt.\%}$ Zn– EGME solution. Commercial porous $\alpha{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ tubes of $4\mathrm{mm}$ outer diameter (OD), $3\mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter (ID), and $100\mathrm{nm}$ nominal pore size were purchased from Hyflux Ltd. Co. The tubes were cut to $60\mathrm{mm}$ in length and washed sequentially with distilled water and ethanol several times. The tubes were then dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ and calcined at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . The internal surface of the cleaned $\alpha{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ tube was coated with the above solution by dip-coating before drying at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . This was repeated twice to obtain a thin
$Z\mathrm{nO}$ layer. The tube was then calcined at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $2\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.2 Growth of (002)-oriented nanosheet membrane
The porous ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ tube coated with ZnO NPs was wrapped with Teflon tape before placement in the synthesis solution, comprising $\mathrm{\bIm{:}N H_{4}O H}$ :methanol (MeOH):toluene (PhMe) with the molar composition of 1:1:45:45, at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . This activation treatment produced nucleation sites for the growth of a uniform nanosheet membrane. The tube was then removed from the oven and rinsed for several minutes with a ligand solution containing $_{1\mathrm{g}}$ of bIm dissolved in $100\mathrm{mLMeOH}$ to remove random deposits and guarantee the presence of oriented nanosheet precursors for the growth of an oriented nanosheet membrane. The treated tube was then placed in the synthesis solution at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the desired growth time. After the synthesis, the membrane was removed, immersed in MeOH for 2 days, thoroughly rinsed with $\mathrm{\bf{MeOH},}$ , dried at room temperature overnight, and stored in a desiccator for later use.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Formation of the oriented nanosheet membrane with varying synthesis time
To probe the nanosheet growth process from ZnO NPs, time-dependent reactions were further performed. As shown in Figs. 2(a)–2(c), early in the reaction $(<0.5\mathrm{h}).$ , the surfaces of the ZnO NPs are coated with nanosheet precursors (Fig. 2(a)). With prolonged reaction time $(3\mathrm{h})$ , the nanosheet precursors grow, forming continuous layers on the surfaces of the $Z\mathrm{nO}$ NPs (Fig. 2(b)). At this stage, the nanosheet size is ${\sim}200\mathrm{nm}$ , whereas the ZnO NP diameter is decreased from ${\sim}50$ to $20\mathrm{nm}$ . As the reaction proceeds, the nanosheets become larger and the ZnO NPs become smaller until the NPs are completely converted to sheets (Fig. 2(c)). These results suggest that the nanosheets are mainly formed by the localized self-conversion of the $Z_{\mathrm{nO}}~\mathrm{NPs},$ , rather than by the dissolution of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ and following ZIF crystallization. This localized self-conversion phenomenon is important for further membrane fabrication, because it allows the direct growth of the nanosheet on a substrate, rather than in a solution. The crystallinity of the materials obtained is identical to that of $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{bIm})_{4},$ as determined by powder $x$ -ray diffraction (PXRD, Fig. 2(e)). The selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern of the nanosheet along the $c$ axis is shown in Fig. 2(d). The result corresponds to the diffraction pattern for the $(h k0)$ planes of the nanosheet, showing that the nanosheets are highly crystalline with the same in-plane crystal structure as the pristine bulk material [24, 29]. An atomic force microscopy (AFM) image (Fig. 2(f)) shows that the resulting nanosheets, cast on a Si wafer, have square lateral dimensions of ${\tilde{\mathbf{\Gamma}}}^{1}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and thicknesses of $20{-}25\mathrm{nm}$ , with an aspect ratio of ${>}40$ .
Moreover, our experimental results show that, during the synthesis, both the treatment of the tube in the synthesis solution for activation and the subsequent washing step greatly influence the growth orientation and quality of the nanosheet membranes. As in our previous report [20], the activation of the ZnO NPs on the tube generated nucleation sites as seeds, favoring the growth of a continuous and dense ZIF membrane. The rinsing of the activated surface with MeOH may wash away active sites with differently oriented crystallographic planes, thus avoiding multidirectional growth of the nanosheet membrane and guaranteeing the preparation of highly oriented 2D nanosheet ZIF membranes.
3.2 Effect of ammonia concentration on the formation of the oriented nanosheet membrane
Generally, $Z\mathrm{nO}$ NPs or nanorods can be solvothermally transformed into Zn-ZIF crystals as previously reported [16, 20]. However, these ZIF synthesis procedures often yield agglomerated powders comprising isotropic micrometer-sized crystals or non-dispersible nonnanosheet structured ZIF nanocrystals. Thus, the conventional methods promote the growth of randomly oriented ZIF membranes on ZnO seed crystals, regardless of the seed crystals’ orientations on the substrates [32, 33]. As a result, the ZIF channels in various directions are randomly oriented within the membranes, creating many discontinuous channels and local mismatches in crystal orientation. The resulting membranes naturally show low gas permeabilities. With our proposed method, $\mathrm{NH_{4}O H}$ is critical as a modulator in preventing growth along directions other than (002). As shown in Fig. 3, with no or lowconcentration (0.25-M) ammonia in the synthesis solution, tetrahedral ZIF crystals are deposited on the ZnO-modified substrate, similar to conventional ZIF powders containing isotropic microscale crystals, and no nanosheets are formed. The PXRD pattern of the obtained crystals matches well with the simulated ZIF-11 XRD structure, indicating that the isostructure belongs to the 3D ZIF-11 (Fig. 3(e)) [34–36]. When the ammonia concentration is increased from 0.5 to $1.0\mathrm{M},$ compact ZIF membranes with nanosheet morphologies are formed on the substrates (Figs. 3(c) and 3(d)) and show XRD pattern features typical of highly oriented growth (Fig. 3(e)). In order to further examine the synthesis solution with an ammonia concentration that favors the production of multilamellar ZIF crystals, we placed ZnO particles as substrates in the synthesis solution containing 1.0-M ammonia as nuclei for bulk-type ZIF crystals. The obtained crystals possess well-defined cubic morphologies and each crystal contains multiple closely packed lamellae, as shown in Fig. 3(f). PXRD patterns of these obtained crystals match well with the simulated pattern of ZIF-11 with RHO topology (Fig. S3 in the ESM) [35, 36]. The multilamellar ZIF crystals are isostructural to a previously determined MOF (Fig. $3(\mathrm{g)}^{\cdot}$ ) (no. 675375, Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre (CCDC)). Thus, $\mathrm{NH_{4}O H}$ is significant in the formation of ZIF nanosheets or membranes. At high concentrations of $\mathrm{NH_{4}O H,}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ may compete with the bIm ligand to coordinate with the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ produced from the $Z_{\mathrm{{nONPs}}}$ on the substrate to form $\mathrm{[}Z\mathrm{n(NH_{3})_{4}]^{2+}}$ coordination compounds, impeding the formation of the 3Dstructured MOF (ZIF-11) [35, 36]. This coordination compound is likely to form ZIF precursors in the synthesis solution. During the growth process, the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ in $\mathrm{[}Z\mathrm{n(NH_{3})_{4}]^{2+}}$ may be substituted by bIm to form
Zn-bIm tetrahedral structures with four-membered rings by the coordination of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ with bIm and thereafter form 2D $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{bIm})_{4}$ layers. Thus, the $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}O H}$ concentration of the synthesis solution dramatically influences and determines the formation of ZIF nanosheet structures during ZIF membrane growth.
## 3.3 Growth of nanosheet membranes of different layer numbers or thicknesses
Growth conditions, especially the synthesis time, are critical in determining the thickness of the ZIF nanosheet membrane. An ultrathin nanosheet membrane that demonstrates excellent separation performance is highly desired [25]. Therefore, the growth of nanosheet membranes with different thicknesses over different synthesis times was conducted to manipulate the number of nanosheet layers and the membrane thickness. Figures $4(\mathsf{a}){-}4(\mathsf{c})$ show the SEM images of the nanosheet membranes obtained at different growth times. From the cross-sectional images, the specimens display clear lamellar structures of stacked layers. With increased synthesis time, the layer number in the ZIF nanosheet membrane increases from 2 layers at $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to 20 layers at $5\mathrm{h},$ thus increasing the corresponding membrane thickness from 50 to $450\mathrm{nm},$ , respectively. All fabricated ZIF nanosheets exhibit strong preferential orientations along the basal planes and form thin and uniform membranes with closely stacked oriented multilayers. The thickness of each single ZIF nanosheet layer is ${\sim}25\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4(a)). This is in agreement with the result reported by Gascon et al. [26]. They prepared $\mathrm{Cu}1,4\cdot$ -benzenedicarboxylate MOF single-layer nanosheets, also ${\sim}25\mathrm{nm}$ thick, for incorporation into polymer matrices to form a highperformance composite membrane. XRD patterns of all the membranes of differing thicknesses show only one reflection, indexed to the (002) crystallographic planes of the ZIF structure (Fig. 4(d)). This plane is perpendicular to the pore openings of the substrate, greatly favoring gas permeation. None of the additional Bragg diffractions observed from the bulk counterpart are detected, indicating the successful growth of highly oriented ZIF membranes containing only nanosheets. According to the XRD pattern and the corresponding crystallographic data, the 3D crystalline structure of the ZIF nanosheet is achieved on the surface modified with $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ NPs (no. 675375, CCDC). The pore direction of the $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(b I m)_{4}}$ consisting of 2D layers is in agreement with that of the substrate. The aperture size of the ZIF is estimated to be $0.21\mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Fig. 4(e).
## 3.4 Gas permeation and separation by the nanosheet membrane
The gas separation properties of the fabricated $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bIm})_{4}2\mathrm{D}$ nanosheet membranes were investigated for $H_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separations. The gas permeation and separation results are presented in Fig. 5. As shown in Fig. 5(a), the permeance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ through the $50\mathrm{-nm}$ -thick nanosheet membrane reaches $2.04~\times~10^{-7}~\mathrm{\mol{\cdot}m^{-2}{\cdot}s^{-1}{\cdot}P a^{-1}}$ . This high permeance is attributed to the low thickness of the nanosheet membrane as well as the preferential growth along the [002] direction. The permeance values of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are much lower than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ confirming that the membrane is continuous, lacking pinholes, and highly compact. The ideal selectivities reach 53, 67, and 90 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H_{2}/C H_{4}},$ respectively (Fig. 5(a)). In order to test the separation performance and membrane stability at high temperature, the longterm gas separation performance of the $50\mathrm{-nm}$ -thick nanosheet membrane for binary gas mixtures at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was tested and the results are shown in Figs. 5(b) and 5(c). Both the gas permeance values and the separation factors remain unchanged over the course of $100\mathrm{h},$ indicating that the prepared nanosheet membrane has excellent thermal and operating stability. This stability may arise from the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanolayer, which may anchor the ZIF nanosheets and prevent peel-off [14, 20].
The comparison of our nanosheet membrane with other MOF membranes reported so far in $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}/\mathrm{{CO}}_{2}$ separation performance is shown in Fig. 5(d) and Table S2 in the ESM. The black solid and dashed lines represent the 2008 upper bound of polymeric membranes and the 2010 upper bound of microporous inorganic membranes for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation [48, 49]. Our nanosheet-structured membrane shows much better performance than the other MOF membranes reported to date and exceeds the latest Robeson upper-bound separation performance for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The separation performances demonstrate that the nanosheet membrane produced using the present strategy is of high quality.
As stated above, the nanosheet membrane thickness could be easily controlled by changing the synthesis time. Thus, the effect of nanosheet membrane thickness on separation was also investigated, as shown in Fig. 6. The thicker membranes exhibit lower selectivities as well as lower $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance values, likely because of the increasing stacked nanosheet slippage with increasing membrane thickness, which would cause misalignments of the pores in different nanosheets. This is in agreement with the results reported by Yang et al. [25].
To demonstrate the versatility of our novel ammonia-assisted $Z\mathrm{nO}$ self-conversion strategy, we attempted to prepare other Zn-MOF nanosheet membranes. Both $Z n\ 1,4$ -benzenedicarboxylate and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate nanosheet membranes were successfully fabricated on porous supports using a similar procedure, as shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM. Therefore, this novel technique can be extended to prepare other Zn-based MOF nanosheet membranes. Further study is currently underway in our laboratory.
## 4 Conclusions
A direct-growth technique with localized $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ selfconversion and ammonium hydroxide assistance is developed to fabricate highly oriented ultrathin 2D nanosheet ZIF membranes on porous tubular supports. By employing a thin layer of $Z\mathrm{nO}$ NPs coated on a substrate with the help of $\mathrm{NH_{4}O H}$ as a modulator, both growth and anchoring sites for the highly oriented growth in the (002) direction of a ZIF nanosheet membrane are provided. Moreover, the membrane thickness can be easily controlled via this growth method by adjusting the reaction time. The abovementioned advantages of this facile ZIF 2D nanosheet membrane fabrication technique suggest that it has great potential for use in large-scale molecular sieving separation applications. In addition, this strategy can be used not only for the fabrication of ZIF nanosheet membranes on porous tubular substrates, but also for thin-film deposition on other functional substrates towards applications in catalysis, sensing, and microelectronics. The method developed here exemplifies a novel route for preparing ultrathin 2D MOF nanomaterials on supports because it is simple, universal, and scalable.
